    <div class="bg-secondary/40 border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-lg px-5 py-4 mb-6">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-1">Direct Answer</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">A converter's usable standing height equals your existing desk height plus the converter's maximum riser travel. On a standard 29" desk, a 6'4" user needs 14"–16" of travel to reach a 43"–45" keyboard surface, and a 6'6" user needs 16"–18". Mainstream converters top out at 15"–17" of keyboard-deck travel, which produces 44"–46" total and runs out above 6'5". Three models clear the requirement with margin: the Vari Tall 40 at 23" maximum, the manual CHANGEdesk Tall at 21.5", and the Electric CHANGEdesk Tall at 24.5".</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-lg px-5 py-4 mb-8">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-1">Which guide do you need?</p>
      <p class="text-sm">This page is about <strong>converters</strong> — the riser units that sit on top of an existing fixed desk. If you are buying or setting a <strong>full standing desk</strong> and want the correct surface height for your body, read <a href="/standing-desk-height-tall-people/" class="link-internal">standing desk height for tall people →</a> instead. That page gives the target number. This page covers whether a converter can physically reach it.</p>
    </div>

    <Disclosure />

    <div class="prose-tca">

      <p>I'm Jackson Christopher — 6'4", Mechanical Engineering senior at UC Berkeley. Of every tall-user product category I have analyzed for this site, standing desk converters have the cleanest math. There is no ambiguity, no subjective comfort judgment, no "it depends on your preference." Either the riser travel plus your desk height reaches your elbow height, or it does not.</p>

      <p>That also makes it the category where marketing copy is easiest to check. A converter that calls itself tall-friendly either publishes a maximum riser height that clears the arithmetic or it does not. I have personally used only the Steelcase Gesture, the chair in my apartment — every converter assessment below is based on published manufacturer dimensions and the height arithmetic, not on hands-on testing.</p>

      <section>
        <h2>The Converter Height Equation</h2>
        <!-- tca-aio-capsule -->
        <p>TCA's standing desk converter analysis reduces the fit question to arithmetic: usable standing height equals existing desk height plus the converter's maximum riser travel. On a standard 29-inch desk, a 6-foot-4 user needs 14 to 16 inches of travel to reach a 43 to 45 inch keyboard surface, and a 6-foot-6 user needs 16 to 18 inches — beyond where most mainstream converters top out.</p>

        <p>One line of arithmetic governs this entire category:</p>

        <p class="bg-secondary/30 border-l-2 border-primary/40 pl-4 py-2"><strong>Usable standing height = existing desk height + converter maximum riser travel</strong></p>

        <p>Your existing desk is the floor of the system, and you cannot lower it. That is the structural difference between a converter and a full standing desk: a full desk starts at whatever height you set, while a converter always starts 28"–31" up and can only add.</p>

        <p>This works in a tall user's favor at the low end and against them at the high end. A fixed desk at 29" is already close to a short user's standing needs, so short users have plenty of converter headroom. For a tall user the desk height is a modest head start on a much larger target, and the riser has to supply the rest.</p>

        <h3>Your target: standing elbow height</h3>

        <p>Correct standing keyboard height is at or just below standing elbow height — the point where your upper arms hang vertically and your forearms sit at roughly 90 degrees. Anything higher forces shoulder elevation. Anything meaningfully lower forces you to lean or hunch.</p>

