        <p class="text-sm"><em>Elbow heights are anthropometric estimates and vary by ±1"–2" with torso-to-leg ratio. Measure your own using the procedure below before buying.</em></p>

        <p class="bg-secondary/30 border-l-2 border-primary/40 pl-4 py-1 text-sm italic">Standing desk converter height for tall users is determined by one equation: existing desk height plus maximum riser travel. On a 29" desk, a 6'4" user requires 14"–16" of riser travel to reach a 43"–45" keyboard surface, and a 6'6" user requires 16"–18". Mainstream converters provide 15"–17" of keyboard-deck travel, producing a 44"–46" ceiling that fails above 6'5".</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Why Most Converters Fail Tall Users — Two Separate Reasons</h2>

        <h3>Failure mode one: the keyboard deck runs out</h3>

        <p>Most mainstream converters advertise 15"–17" of rise. Run the equation on a 29" desk and that yields a 44"–46" keyboard surface. Honest assessment: that actually works for 6'0" through about 6'4". The category is not universally broken.</p>

        <p>It breaks at 6'5" and above, where 44"–46" is exactly the target rather than comfortably above it — meaning you are running the mechanism at its absolute maximum with zero adjustment margin. That matters mechanically. A Z-lift or parallelogram riser is least stable at full extension, where the linkage angles are shallowest and any keystroke force produces the largest deflection at the top. Buying a converter that only just reaches your height means using it at its wobbliest setting every single time.</p>

        <p>It also breaks earlier if your desk is short. A 28" desk costs you an inch, which pushes 6'4" into the same no-margin situation. Measure your desk. Fixed desks in the wild run from 28" to 31" and people routinely assume 30" when they own a 28.5".</p>

        <h3>Failure mode two: the monitor deck is far too low</h3>

        <p>This is the one almost nobody checks, and it defeats nearly every converter regardless of brand or price.</p>

        <p>On a converter, the monitor sits on a top deck that is typically only 5"–7" above the keyboard deck. But the correct top-of-screen height for a tall user is at or just below standing eye level. At 6'4", standing eye level is roughly 71"–72". With a keyboard surface at 45", the top of the monitor should be roughly 24"–26" above the typing surface. A monitor sitting on a deck 6" up, with its own screen adding maybe 12"–14", puts the top around 18"–20" above the keyboard — still 5"–8" short.</p>

        <p>The mechanical consequence is the same forward-head posture that a low seated monitor produces: sustained cervical flexion loading the upper trapezius and cervical extensors for hours. Standing does not fix a neck angle problem; it only changes which muscles fatigue.</p>

        <p>The practical answer is that a converter is a keyboard-height solution, not a monitor-height solution. Pair one with a clamp-mount arm and you have both. Details on the arm side: <a href="/monitor-arm-tall-people/" class="link-internal">monitor arms for tall people — height range and reach →</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Standing Desk Converters That Reach Tall Height</h2>

        <p>Three models publish riser maximums that clear the arithmetic for users through 6'7" with real margin. Total heights below assume a 29" desk — adjust for your measured desk height.</p>

