        <h3>Vari Tall 40 — The Only Converter Marketed by User Height</h3>

        <p><strong>Riser range:</strong> 4.75"–23" across 9 settings &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Total on a 29" desk:</strong> ~33.75"–52" &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Approx. price:</strong> $350–$450</p>

        <p>The Vari Tall 40 is unusual in that its own product listing states it is built for users up to 6'6" — an explicit height claim rather than an inferred one. Based on published dimensions, the 23" maximum riser on a 29" desk produces a roughly 52" keyboard surface, which is 5"–7" above what even a 6'7" user needs. You will never run this at maximum, which is exactly the point: it operates in the stable middle of its travel.</p>

        <p>The 9 discrete settings are worth understanding. A stepped mechanism is not infinitely adjustable, so your ideal height may fall between two settings. With 23" spread over 9 stops, the steps average roughly 2.3" apart. For a 6'4" user targeting 43"–45", that 2" target window will contain a setting, but it is worth confirming rather than assuming. Stepped mechanisms trade fine adjustment for repeatability — you land on the same height every time, which a friction-based gas lift does not guarantee.</p>

        <p>The 40" wide surface is also relevant to tall users specifically. Longer arms want wider elbow spacing, and a narrow 30"–32" converter deck forces the forearms inward. This is the same shoulder-position issue that armrest width creates in chairs.</p>

