        <p>The manual CHANGEdesk Tall delivers 21.5" of travel at roughly a third of the Vari's price, which makes it the strongest value in the category on pure height-per-dollar. On a 29" desk it reaches approximately 50.5" — clearing 6'6" requirements with about 3" of margin.</p>

        <p>The separate keyboard tray is the specification that matters most here, and it is easy to overlook. A converter with an integrated single-surface design forces the keyboard and monitor to the same height. A converter with a dedicated keyboard tray lets the typing surface sit lower than the monitor platform, which is the correct relationship: keyboard at elbow height, screen far above it. For tall users, whose keyboard-to-eye vertical gap is the largest of any user group, that separation is worth more than it sounds.</p>

        <p>Manual operation is the tradeoff. Raising and lowering a loaded converter by hand several times a day is a friction point that determines whether you actually alternate postures or just leave it in one position. In our reading of tall-user reports across r/ergonomics and r/StandingDesk, abandoned sit-stand routines correlate far more with transition effort than with height fit.</p>

