        <p>The motorized version offers the highest ceiling in this comparison at 24.5" of travel, reaching approximately 53.5" on a 29" desk. That covers 6'7" with roughly 5" of margin and is the only model here with genuine headroom above 6'6".</p>

        <p>Note the higher floor: the minimum riser is 6.75" rather than 4.5". On a 29" desk that puts the lowest position around 35.75", which is 6"–7" above a normal seated keyboard height. If you plan to type seated with the converter in place, you will need to raise your chair substantially or use the desk surface beside the unit. Verify how you intend to work seated before buying — this is the single most common regret with high-floor converters.</p>

        <p>Motorized transition removes the effort barrier that undermines manual units. Confidence on this model is medium rather than high: the published range is clear, but motorized converters carry more variance in load rating, travel speed, and long-term reliability than a purely mechanical linkage, and those factors are not resolvable from specifications alone.</p>

