      <section>
        <h2>How to Calculate the Converter Height You Need</h2>

        <h3>Step 1: Measure standing elbow height</h3>
        <p>Stand upright in the shoes you actually work in. Let your upper arms hang vertically, bend your forearms to 90 degrees, and measure from the floor to the underside of your elbow. Do not estimate from the table if you can measure — torso-to-leg ratio moves this number by 1"–2" at the same standing height.</p>

        <h3>Step 2: Measure your desk</h3>
        <p>Floor to desktop. Write down the actual number. This is where most converter mistakes originate, because the difference between a 28" and a 31" desk is three full inches of riser requirement.</p>

        <h3>Step 3: Subtract</h3>
        <p>Required travel = elbow height minus desk height. Optionally subtract another 1"–2" if you prefer the keyboard just below elbow level, which many people do because it opens the wrist angle slightly.</p>

        <h3>Step 4: Add margin, and check the right deck</h3>
        <p>Add 2"–3" so you are not operating at maximum extension, where stability is lowest. Then confirm the manufacturer's published maximum refers to the keyboard deck, not the monitor deck. On most designs those differ by 5"–7", and a listing that quotes the higher number will overstate real typing height.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What a Converter Cannot Fix</h2>

        <p>Being clear about the limits keeps this from being a purchase you regret:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Monitor height.</strong> Covered above. Budget for a monitor arm or riser alongside the converter, not instead of one.</li>
          <li><strong>Seated ergonomics.</strong> A converter does nothing for how you sit. If your seated posture is the actual problem, the chair is where the money should go — see <a href="/office-chairs-for-tall-people/" class="link-internal">chairs for tall people →</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Wrist angle at height.</strong> Raising the typing surface without changing its tilt can force wrist extension, particularly on units without a dedicated tray. A negative-tilt tray restores the neutral angle: <a href="/keyboard-tray-tall-people/" class="link-internal">keyboard trays for tall users at high desks →</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Desk depth.</strong> A converter consumes 20"–24" of desk depth and pushes the monitor closer to you. On a shallow 24" desk, the resulting viewing distance can drop below the comfortable 20"–30" range.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>If you can replace the desk instead, do. A full electric standing desk moves the keyboard and monitor together, typically reaches 48"–51", and eliminates both failure modes at once. The converter exists for the case where the desk is not yours to replace.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>How tall does a standing desk converter need to be for a 6'4" person?</h3>
        <p>Standing elbow height at 6'4" is approximately 45", so the keyboard surface belongs at 43"–45". On a 29" desk that requires 14"–16" of riser travel; on a 31" desk, 12"–14". Models with 21"–24" of travel, such as the CHANGEdesk Tall and Vari Tall 40, clear the requirement with margin instead of running at maximum extension.</p>

        <h3>Why do most standing desk converters fail tall users?</h3>
        <p>Two reasons. The keyboard deck on a mainstream converter rises 15"–17", producing a 44"–46" surface on a 29" desk — adequate through about 6'4" and short above 6'5". Separately, the monitor deck sits only 5"–7" above the keyboard deck, while a 6'4" user needs the screen top 24"–26" above the typing surface. Almost no converter solves the second problem without an added arm or riser.</p>

        <h3>What is the total height of a converter on a standard desk?</h3>
        <p>Desk height plus maximum riser travel. On a 29" desk: Vari Tall 40 reaches ~52", manual CHANGEdesk Tall ~50.5", Electric CHANGEdesk Tall ~53.5", and a typical 17"-travel converter ~46". Because fixed desks range from 28" to 31", measure yours before applying any of these numbers.</p>

        <h3>Is a converter or a full standing desk better for a tall person?</h3>
        <p>A full electric desk is ergonomically better above 6'2" because it raises keyboard and monitor together and typically reaches 48"–51". A converter is the right call when the desk cannot be replaced — a rental, a dorm, a shared office. Accept that you will also need a monitor solution on top.</p>

        <h3>Does a converter work on a 28" desk for someone over 6'5"?</h3>
        <p>Only a tall-specific one. At 6'5" the target keyboard surface is 44"–46", which on a 28" desk needs 16"–18" of travel; at 6'7", 18"–20". Standard 15"–17" converters fall short. The Electric CHANGEdesk Tall at 24.5" and the Vari Tall 40 at 23" both clear it comfortably.</p>

        <h3>Do I still need a monitor arm if I use a converter?</h3>
        <p>Almost certainly, yes. The converter's top deck raises the monitor only 5"–7" above the keyboard, leaving the screen roughly 5"–8" below correct standing eye height for a 6'4" user. A clamp-mount arm with sufficient vertical range closes the gap and lets the screen follow you between sitting and standing.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
        <p>Converters are the most spec-transparent tall-user category on this site. Measure your elbow height, measure your desk, subtract, add 2"–3" of margin, and buy a unit whose keyboard deck clears the result. On a 29" desk that means 14"–16" of travel at 6'4" and 16"–18" at 6'6" — and the three models above deliver 21.5", 23", and 24.5" respectively.</p>
        <p>Then plan the monitor separately. Fixing typing height while leaving the screen 6" too low simply relocates the strain from the wrists to the neck.</p>
      </section>

